let __v_399 = Value::from("The photo is the part that gets an application sent back: plain light background, no glasses, mouth closed and a neutral face, taken in the last month, with no shadow on the face. An online form wants a square digital file rather than a photo of a print, and the size rule is on your government's site.");
frame.store("_PASSPORT_PHOTO_RULES", __v_399.clone())?;
let __v_400 = Value::from("A photo booth is the quickest: about £5 to £8 for a strip, and most are in a supermarket, a station or a big chemist. A chemist, a camera shop such as Snappy Snaps or Timpson, or a Post Office takes them for £8 to £12 and checks the photo as it goes, which is worth it for a baby. Whichever you use, the photo still has to be a plain light background, no glasses, mouth closed, taken in the last month.");
frame.store("_PASSPORT_PHOTO_PLACES", __v_400.clone())?;
let __v_401 = Value::from("Yes - for an online application a phone photo is fine and it is what most people do: stand them against a plain light wall in daylight, so the background is bare, with no flash and no shadow, get someone else to take it from about arm's length at the height of the face, keep the shoulders in, and crop it square. The form tells you whether it passes before you pay, so take a few and use the best.");
frame.store("_PASSPORT_PHOTO_AT_HOME", __v_401.clone())?;
let __v_402 = Value::from("About £5 to £8 for a strip of four from a booth, and £8 to £12 in a chemist, a camera shop or a Post Office, where it is checked as it is taken. A digital code for an online application is usually included; a passport office's own service costs more again because the fee is for the check.");
frame.store("_PASSPORT_PHOTO_PRICE", __v_402.clone())?;
